   “Why?”
   “They didn’t say.” He looked at the dark window. “I used to think it
was for safety. Then I started graphing it.”
   He pulled a laptop from under the table. A spreadsheet, seven
weeks of it, rows and rows. A color-coded chart.
   “Every Thursday,” he said. “Nine-forty p.m. They run a test tone.
The whole block, speakers and app at once. Ninety seconds, the first
Thursday. Three minutes the second. Five, then six, then eight.” He
tapped the screen. “Last Thursday was eight.”
   “And then?”
   “And then for a day, the block’s quiet. The incidents drop. Like, way
down, nobody fights, it’s nice.” His mouth twisted. “Then they come
back worse. Three days of worse. Then Thursday again.” He pointed at
a green bar on the chart. “The dashboard calls the quiet day yield.”
   “A pulse,” I said.
   “I don’t know what it is. I just count.” He swiped. “The café is the
only place I can’t sort. The whole block’s a flat line with spikes, except
there’s one dot that does the opposite. It’s loud and nobody fights.”
   “Mr. Rodríguez’s.”
   “I figured it was a bug.” He looked at me. “It’s you, isn’t it. You and
the drum guy.”
   Manny, beside me, raised a hand like a student.
   “They’re going to do eleven on the thirteenth,” said Danny. “That’s
what the ramp is. They’re not doing a ceremony. They’re doing the last
step.” He looked at his ear in the dark of the monitor. “I think it’s a
dress rehearsal. I think you’ve been in the audience for seven weeks.”
   I looked at the chart on the screen. Five pulses, getting longer. A
staircase, built in the dark, one step a week.



